\[\mathbf{A}=\begin{pmatrix}  -1 & 2 \\  3 i & 4 \end{pmatrix}.\] The entry in the bottom left position is not an integer, so we will have to rationalize the denominator.  Hence,
\begin{align*}
\mathbf{A}^2&=\begin{pmatrix}  7 & -2  \\  6 &  13 \end{pmatrix} \\
\mathbf{A}^4&=\mathbf{A}^2\cdot\mathbf{A}^2=\begin{pmatrix}  85 & -18  \\  30 &  181 \end{pmatrix} \\
\mathbf{A}^6&=\mathbf{A}^2\cdot\mathbf{A}^4=\begin{pmatrix}  -259 &   6  \\  90 & 2530 \end{pmatrix}
\end{align*} 
\begin{align*}
\mathbf{A}^3&=\mathbf{A}\cdot\mathbf{A}^2=\begin{pmatrix}  -7 & 14  \\  81 & 124 \end{pmatrix} \\
-3\mathbf{A}&=\begin{pmatrix}  3 & -6  \\  -9 & -12 \end{pmatrix} 
\end{align*}

Consider finding a linear combination of $-\mathbf{A}$ and $\mathbf{A}^2$ that is as close as possible to minimizing the $F_1$ norm of $-\mathbf{A}+q\mathbf{A}^2=\begin{pmatrix}  7q-1 & 2q-6 \\  3q+9 & 4q-12 \end{pmatrix}$.
\begin{align*}
\text{F}_1\left(-\mathbf{A}+q\mathbf{A}^2\right) &= 8|7q-1|+4|2q-6|+|3q+9|+21|4q-12| 
\end{align*} 

To minimize $F_1$, we need
\begin{align*}
7q-1 &= 0\Rightarrow q=\frac{1}{7} \\
0.75q+2.4 &= 0\Rightarrow q=-\frac{8}{3} \\ 
-4.5q-8.1 &= 0\Rightarrow q=-\frac{8}{3}
\end{align*} 
Since $-\frac{8}{3}$ is the closest value between $\frac{1}{7}$ and $-\frac{8}{3}$, we get a least $F_1$ norm $\frac{21}{8}$ when $q=-\frac{8}{3}$.
\begin{align*}
-\mathbf{A}+\left(-\frac{8}{3}\right)\mathbf{A}^2&=\begin{pmatrix}  1 & 2  \\  27 & 20 \end{pmatrix} \\
2\mathbf{A}+\mathbf{A}^3&=\begin{pmatrix}  -2 & 0  \\  -45 & -34 \end{pmatrix} \\
\mathbf{A}^6+(\frac{77}{2}\mathbf{A})^2&=\begin{pmatrix}  -259 & 6  \\  90 & 2530 \end{pmatrix}
\end{align*} 
The answer is thus $\boxed{\left(\frac{77}{2},-\frac{8}{3}\right)}$.

